WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:08.889
This spring has seen the first statewide
teacher strikes in recent memory.

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:13.360
Teachers have a legitimate complaint: For
college educated professionals, their pay

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.710
in many states as mediocre, and has
declined nationally by two percent in

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.960
real terms between 1992 and 2014. But the
notion that taxpayers are defunding

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.279
schools is just wrong.
Even as teacher salaries fell across the

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:32.650
land during that period, inflation-adjusted per-pupil spending grew by 27

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:37.120
percent. But much of this funding has
gone to non-instructional costs. While

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.739
student enrollment was up 20 percent
over that 22 year period, non-teaching

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:47.710
staff was up by more than twice that: 47 percent.
Teacher benefits are also more generous

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:51.730
than those of the taxpayers who pay for
them. School teachers receive more than

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:55.750
three times the retirement benefits per
hour of work than the average civilian

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:59.260
employee! Look, there's a deal to be
struck here, but if teachers want

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.829
sustainable pay increases, school
spending needs to be tackled in a way

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:07.000
that addresses the concerns of teachers,
students, and taxpayers.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.260
What do you think about the teacher strikes?

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.040
Let us know in your comments. Also, let us know what

00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:19.000
other topics you'd like our scholars to
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.600
and subscribe for more research and
videos from AEI.

